export const BANDITS_MODULES = [
  {
    id: 'mab_problem',
    title: 'Multi-Armed Bandit Problem',
    subtitle: 'Exploration-exploitation tradeoff, regret formulation, stochastic vs adversarial MAB',
    difficulty: 'foundational',
    estimatedMin: 35,
    tags: ['bandit', 'exploration', 'regret', 'exploration-exploitation', 'stochastic'],
    summary: `The multi-armed bandit is the canonical formulation of sequential decision making under uncertainty: at each round, choose an arm (action), observe a reward, and update beliefs. The tension is that pulling the arm with the highest estimated reward (exploitation) forecloses learning about potentially better arms (exploration). Regret — the cumulative gap between your policy's expected reward and the oracle optimum — is the right objective because it jointly captures both accuracy of reward estimation and cumulative cost of sub-optimal choices, unlike point accuracy which ignores sequential opportunity cost. The stochastic MAB assumes rewards are i.i.d. from fixed distributions; the adversarial MAB makes no distributional assumptions and requires algorithms that are robust to worst-case reward sequences.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'Regret is the right metric, not reward accuracy. Pseudo-regret R_T = T * μ* − Σ_t μ_{a_t} measures the cumulative gap between the optimal arm\'s expected reward and your policy\'s expected rewards. Accuracy of reward estimation could be high while regret is catastrophic (e.g., if you waste pulls confirming a bad arm is bad).',
      'Expected regret decomposition: R_T = Σ_{a≠a*} Δ_a · E[N_a(T)] where Δ_a = μ* − μ_a is the suboptimality gap for arm a and N_a(T) is the number of times you pull arm a. To minimize regret you must minimize pulls on sub-optimal arms — but identifying which arms are sub-optimal requires exploration.',
      'Lai-Robbins lower bound (1985): for any consistent algorithm (one that achieves sub-polynomial regret for every instance), expected regret satisfies E[R_T] ≥ Σ_{a: Δ_a > 0} Δ_a / KL(μ_a, μ*) · ln T. This is Ω(log T) and cannot be beaten asymptotically. Algorithms achieving O(log T) are optimal.',
      'Worst-case regret: even in the stochastic case, minimax regret is Θ(√(KT)) over the class of K-arm Bernoulli bandits — you cannot know Δ in advance. EXP3 achieves this in the adversarial setting. UCB-type algorithms achieve O(log T) for specific instances but √(KT) worst-case.',
      'Stochastic MAB: rewards are i.i.d. from fixed but unknown distributions (e.g., Bernoulli, Gaussian). The optimal arm is fixed. Most practical recommendation/ad problems are approximately stochastic over short windows.',
      'Adversarial MAB: the environment (or adversary) chooses reward sequences after seeing your algorithm (but not your random coin flips). No distributional assumptions. Relevant for strategic actors, financial markets, and robustness requirements.',
      'Production applications: (1) Online ad serving — each ad is an arm, reward is click/conversion; tens of millions of arms with context. (2) Recommendation ranking — which item to surface at position 1; delayed feedback (user clicks after leaving the page). (3) Clinical trials — allocate patients to treatments; ethical pressure to exploit early (response-adaptive randomisation). (4) Hyperparameter tuning (Hyperband) — allocate compute budget across configurations.',
      'Regret vs. accuracy misconception: a model with perfect reward prediction but pure greedy selection has zero exploration — its regret can be linear (O(T)) because it never discovers a better arm it happened to underestimate early. Regret forces the policy, not just the model, to be evaluated.',
      'Finite vs. infinite horizon: most theory is asymptotic (T → ∞). In practice T is finite and instance-dependent factors (Δ values, K) dominate. An algorithm optimal for T = 10^6 may under-perform for T = 1000 where warm-starting priors matter more than asymptotic constants.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: 'You have a news article recommendation system with 500 candidate articles. Each user visit is a bandit round. Why is regret a better objective than classification accuracy of click prediction?',
        a: 'Classification accuracy measures how well the model predicts clicks — it evaluates the model in isolation, not the policy. Regret measures the cumulative opportunity cost of the recommendation policy. A model can have high click prediction accuracy but, if the policy is purely greedy (always recommend the highest-predicted-CTR article), it never explores new articles. If one new article is actually best but had a low initial estimate, greedy policy has linear regret — it never discovers it. Regret forces you to optimize the sequential decision process: the joint accuracy-exploration tradeoff. In production, this translates to: maximizing total clicks (or revenue) over a horizon, not per-round prediction error.',
      },
      {
        q: 'What is the difference between pseudo-regret and expected regret? Which is typically analyzed in theory?',
        a: 'Expected regret (sometimes called "expected regret") is E[Σ_t (μ* − μ_{a_t})], the expectation over randomness in both the rewards and the algorithm\'s random choices. Pseudo-regret is Σ_t (μ* − μ_{a_t}) — the gap using the true best arm mean μ* — and is itself a random variable (over algorithm randomness) whose expectation is expected regret. A stronger notion, high-probability regret, bounds Σ_t (μ* − r_{a_t,t}) — the actual cumulative loss including reward noise. Theory typically analyzes expected pseudo-regret because it factors out reward noise (which is zero-mean and cancels in expectation) and focuses on the sub-optimality of arm selection. The distinction matters in adversarial settings where the reward noise is part of the adversary\'s strategy.',
      },
      {
        q: 'In a clinical trial with 4 treatments and 200 patients total, should you use a bandit algorithm? What ethical constraints interact with regret minimization?',
        a: 'Bandit algorithms (response-adaptive randomisation, e.g., Thompson Sampling) minimize cumulative regret, steering more patients toward the currently-best-estimated treatment as the trial progresses. This is ethically attractive: fewer patients receive inferior treatments. However, there are major tensions: (1) Statistical validity — adaptive designs complicate inference; the final test of H0 requires correcting for the adaptive allocation (group-sequential or permutation tests). (2) Regulatory acceptance — FDA/EMA require pre-specified stopping rules and allocation ratios; pure bandit designs are often not accepted without extensive pre-registration. (3) SUTVA — if patients interact (e.g., infectious disease), treatment assignment of one affects others. (4) Unknown reward delay — in many trials, outcomes are observed months later, making online updating impossible. For a senior MLE answer: bandit algorithms are appropriate for internal rapid-iteration decisions (A/B tests) but require careful statistical design in regulated clinical contexts.',
      },
      {
        q: 'The Lai-Robbins lower bound is Ω(log T). Does this mean no algorithm can do better than O(log T) regret? What assumptions does the bound depend on?',
        a: 'The Lai-Robbins bound says: for any *consistent* algorithm (regret is o(T^α) for every α > 0 and every bandit instance), the expected regret on at least some instances must grow as Ω(log T). So no: you cannot do better than O(log T) regret uniformly across all instances with a consistent algorithm. The bound requires: (1) Stochastic i.i.d. rewards from a parametric family (e.g., Bernoulli, exponential). (2) Consistency of the algorithm. (3) The lower bound is problem-instance-dependent — involves KL divergence between arm distributions. You can achieve O(1) regret on any specific instance if you have a correct prior (Bayesian oracle) but you cannot guarantee this simultaneously across all instances. In the adversarial setting (no distributional assumptions), the lower bound is Ω(√(KT)), not log T.',
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `Regret is the right objective for bandit problems — not reward prediction accuracy — because a perfect reward model paired with a greedy policy still incurs linear regret if it never explores. The Lai-Robbins lower bound (Ω(log T) for stochastic MAB) is the key theoretical anchor: any consistent algorithm must pull suboptimal arms at rate proportional to log T / KL(μ_a, μ*), which is why pure exploitation fails and algorithms like UCB and Thompson Sampling that are designed to match this rate are fundamentally necessary.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'epsilon_greedy',
    title: 'Epsilon-Greedy Exploration',
    subtitle: 'Simplest exploration strategy, annealing schedules, failure modes, non-stationary rewards',
    difficulty: 'foundational',
    estimatedMin: 30,
    tags: ['epsilon-greedy', 'exploration', 'annealing', 'non-stationary'],
    summary: `Epsilon-greedy is the baseline bandit algorithm: with probability ε explore (pull a uniformly random arm) and with probability 1−ε exploit (pull the arm with the highest empirical mean). Despite its simplicity, ε-greedy is surprisingly competitive in practice, especially when arm gaps Δ are small and T is large — because the exploit step is essentially free given the empirical means converge. Its main failure modes are high variance of estimates early on (the uniform exploration wastes budget on clearly inferior arms) and poor adaptation to non-stationary reward distributions where frozen empirical means become stale.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'Pure exploitation (ε=0 greedy) fails because it cannot recover from a bad initialization. If arm A has empirical mean 0.8 (lucky early pulls) and arm B has true mean 0.9 (never pulled enough), greedy stays on A forever — linear regret O(T).',
      'ε-greedy achieves O(log T) regret with proper annealing. Standard result: ε_t = min(1, cK / (d² t)) for constants c, d achieves E[R_T] = O(K log T) regret. The key is that ε → 0 as t → ∞ so exploration decays as confidence grows.',
      'Annealing schedules: (1) 1/t annealing — ε_t = ε_0 / t. Aggressive; may underexplore if ε_0 is too small. (2) 1/√t annealing — slower decay, more exploration early. (3) Cosine annealing or step decay — common in DRL but not theoretically motivated for MAB. (4) ε-first (explore-then-commit): explore all arms equally for √T rounds, then commit to empirical best. Simple, achieves O(√T) regret. Good for batch settings.',
      'Uniform exploration is wasteful. ε-greedy explores all arms equally regardless of their estimated suboptimality. A clearly inferior arm (empirical mean 0.1 vs best 0.9) still receives ε/K fraction of pulls. UCB and TS focus exploration on *uncertain* arms, not random arms — more efficient.',
      'ε-greedy in practice is often competitive because: (1) Real reward gaps Δ are often small and uniform exploration accidentally allocates reasonable budgets. (2) Implementation is trivial — no confidence interval computation, no posterior maintenance. (3) Low computational cost scales to billions of arms with simple hash-based selection.',
      'Non-stationary rewards: fixed ε with a frozen empirical mean fails badly when reward distributions shift (e.g., seasonal CTR changes, content lifecycle). Solutions: (1) Sliding window ε-greedy — only use last W observations for mean estimation. (2) Discounted mean — weight recent observations more heavily: μ̂_a ← γ·μ̂_a + (1−γ)·r. (3) Periodic reset — discard old statistics.',
      'Failure mode: high variance early. With K arms and N total initial pulls, each arm gets only N/K pulls from exploration. If rewards are Bernoulli(0.5), the standard error of the mean is ~0.5/√(N/K). With K=100, N=1000, SE ≈ 0.16 — way too noisy. The exploit step early on picks based on noise, not signal.',
      'ε-first (explore-then-commit) is theoretically cleaner: explore uniformly for m rounds (m = T^{2/3} (K ln K)^{1/3} is optimal), then exploit. Regret = O(T^{2/3} (K ln K)^{1/3}). Worse than UCB\'s O(log T) but simple and useful when exploration and exploitation must be temporally separated (batch settings).',
      'Misconception: "smaller ε is always better." False. If T is short or K is large, insufficient exploration means you never identify the best arm. ε should be tuned to K and T: larger ε for more arms and longer horizons relative to gap sizes.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: 'You deploy ε=0.1 greedy for ad serving with K=50 ads. After 3 months, CTR on most ads has dropped 30% due to creative fatigue, but a new batch of ads was just added. What happens and how do you fix it?',
        a: 'Two problems: (1) Creative fatigue — the empirical means for existing ads reflect historical CTR, not current CTR. The exploit step (90% of traffic) now exploits stale statistics, potentially serving degraded ads. (2) New ads — cold start. New ads have zero pulls, so they never appear in the exploit step; they get only ε/K = 0.1/50 = 0.2% of traffic each, which is far too little to evaluate them quickly. Fix: (a) Discounted empirical mean: μ̂_a ← 0.99·μ̂_a + 0.01·r for recent observations (exponential moving average). (b) Forced exploration: new ads get a forced pull budget (e.g., 100 pulls) before entering the standard pool. (c) Sliding window: recompute means over the last W=7 days only. (d) Switch to Thompson Sampling with a Beta(1,1) prior reset for new ads — uncertainty bonus handles cold start naturally.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Prove informally that ε-greedy with fixed ε has linear regret (O(T)) even with optimal ε.',
        a: 'With fixed ε, the algorithm explores uniformly at rate ε in every round forever. The regret from exploration rounds is: in each exploration round, the expected per-step regret is Σ_a P(pull a)·Δ_a = (1/K)·Σ_a Δ_a = Δ̄ (average suboptimality gap). Over T rounds, exploration regret ≥ ε·T·Δ̄ — linear in T. No matter how small ε is, as long as ε > 0, the exploration term grows linearly. For sub-linear regret you need ε_t → 0 as t → ∞. This is why annealing schedules are required. With ε_t = c/t, the total exploration regret = Σ_t ε_t·Δ̄ = c·Δ̄·Σ_t 1/t = O(log T). The exploit term is O(log T) as well because arm mean estimates converge quickly.',
      },
      {
        q: 'In a production system you A/B test two versions of ε-greedy: (a) ε=0.05, (b) adaptive ε_t = 1/√t. After T=10,000 rounds with K=10 arms, which achieves lower regret and why might (a) still be preferred in engineering practice?',
        a: '(b) achieves lower asymptotic regret: O(K log T) vs O(T) for fixed ε. With T=10,000 and K=10, adaptive ε gives roughly O(10 · 9.2) ≈ O(92) ·constant regret terms, while fixed ε=0.05 gives 0.05·10,000·Δ̄ = 500·Δ̄ regret from exploration alone. However, (a) may be preferred in practice because: (1) Stationarity: ε=0.05 continuously explores, making it robust to reward distribution shifts without code changes. (2) Interpretability: constant exploration rate is easy to reason about and audit. Operations teams understand "5% traffic is exploratory." (3) Tuning stability: 1/√t annealing is sensitive to t=0 initialization — if t resets (system restart, daily cronjob), exploration rate jumps back. (4) In many real systems, T_effective is short per cohort (content has a 1-2 day lifecycle), so asymptotic regret differences don\'t materialize.',
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `Fixed-ε greedy has linear regret because the constant exploration rate ε·T grows unboundedly — sub-linear regret requires ε_t → 0, which is why annealing is not optional but mathematically necessary. In practice, the critical failure modes are the cold-start problem (new arms get ε/K of traffic, often far too little for rapid evaluation) and non-stationary rewards (frozen empirical means become dangerously stale) — both of which require either discounted means or a switch to Thompson Sampling.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'ucb_algorithms',
    title: 'Upper Confidence Bound Algorithms',
    subtitle: 'UCB1, Lai-Robbins optimality, UCB variants, confidence bound construction',
    difficulty: 'intermediate',
    estimatedMin: 45,
    tags: ['UCB', 'UCB1', 'confidence bound', 'optimism', 'KL-UCB', 'MOSS'],
    summary: `UCB algorithms operationalize "optimism in the face of uncertainty": assign each arm an upper confidence bound on its true mean and always pull the arm with the highest UCB. This systematically targets arms that are either high-reward or under-explored (high uncertainty). UCB1 achieves the asymptotically optimal O(log T) regret matching the Lai-Robbins lower bound up to constants. In production, UCB's deterministic nature (no randomness) makes it debuggable and auditable, but naive UCB scales poorly to millions of arms because it requires maintaining per-arm confidence intervals.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'UCB1 algorithm: select arm a_t = argmax_a [μ̂_a + √(2 ln t / N_a(t))] where μ̂_a is the empirical mean of arm a and N_a(t) is the pull count. The √(2 ln t / N_a) term is the confidence bonus — it decays as N_a grows (arm gets explored) and grows as t increases (global pressure to re-explore).',
      'Regret bound for UCB1: E[R_T] ≤ Σ_{a: Δ_a > 0} [8 ln T / Δ_a + (1 + π²/3)·Δ_a]. The 8/Δ_a factor is the pull count contribution; arms with small Δ_a (near-optimal) are pulled more often, which is correct. The bound is O(log T) and matches the Lai-Robbins lower bound in form.',
      'Optimism principle: UCB acts as if each arm\'s mean is at its UCB — the most optimistic plausible value. If the true mean is at the UCB, the arm is pulled. If the true mean is below the UCB (too optimistic), the arm will be pulled but will disappoint, quickly collapsing its UCB. The algorithm "punishes" over-optimism with data.',
      'UCB1-tuned: adds empirical variance to the bonus: UCB = μ̂_a + √(min(1/4, V_a(t)) · ln t / N_a) where V_a = estimated variance + √(2 ln t / N_a). Empirically outperforms UCB1 when reward variance differs across arms.',
      'MOSS (Minimax Optimal Strategy in the Stochastic case): UCB index = μ̂_a + √(max(0, ln(T/(K·N_a))) / N_a). Achieves minimax optimal regret O(√(KT)) over worst-case Bernoulli instances, unlike UCB1 which is instance-optimal but not minimax-optimal.',
      'KL-UCB: instead of Hoeffding-based confidence intervals, uses the KL divergence between empirical distribution and hypothesized distributions. UCB_a = max{q : N_a · KL(μ̂_a, q) ≤ ln t + c ln ln t}. Achieves the Lai-Robbins lower bound with matching constant (KL term). Computationally more expensive but asymptotically tighter.',
      'UCB in practice — discretization for continuous arms: if arms are parameterized continuously (e.g., bid value in ∈ [0,1]), discretize into K = T^{1/3} bins for O(T^{2/3}) regret. Or use kernel UCB / GP-UCB for smooth reward functions with O(T^{(d+1)/(d+2)}) regret in d dimensions.',
      'Computational cost at scale: standard UCB requires argmax over all K arms per round — O(K) per step, O(KT) total. With K=10M arms (web-scale ad serving), this is infeasible. Solutions: (1) Approximate nearest neighbor for UCB scores. (2) Only maintain UCB for arms with sufficient impressions. (3) Hierarchical UCB: tree over arms, UCB selects nodes top-down. (4) Batched updates — recompute UCB scores every B rounds.',
      'Failure mode — reward model misspecification: UCB assumes rewards come from a distribution in the assumed family (Bernoulli for binary, Gaussian for continuous). If rewards are heavy-tailed (Cauchy, Pareto), the confidence intervals are invalid and under-cover, leading to under-exploration. Use robust mean estimators (trimmed mean, median of means) with modified confidence intervals.',
      'Misconception: UCB is optimal. UCB1 is asymptotically instance-optimal for stochastic MAB but Thompson Sampling typically performs better empirically at finite T because the Bayesian update more efficiently uses all reward information, not just the empirical mean and count.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: 'You have K=5 arms with true means [0.9, 0.8, 0.5, 0.3, 0.1]. After t=100 rounds with UCB1, arm 1 has been pulled N_1=30 times and arm 5 has been pulled N_5=5 times. Compute the UCB scores and explain what the algorithm will do.',
        a: 'UCB score = μ̂_a + √(2 ln t / N_a). Assuming empirical means track true means: UCB_1 = 0.9 + √(2·ln(100)/30) = 0.9 + √(9.21/30) = 0.9 + √0.307 ≈ 0.9 + 0.554 = 1.454. UCB_5 = 0.1 + √(2·ln(100)/5) = 0.1 + √(9.21/5) = 0.1 + √1.842 ≈ 0.1 + 1.357 = 1.457. The algorithm pulls arm 5 because its UCB (1.457) slightly exceeds arm 1\'s UCB (1.454). This is the "optimism" at work: arm 5 is under-explored (N=5), so the algorithm gives it the benefit of the doubt. After one more pull of arm 5 reveals a low reward, its UCB drops and the algorithm returns to arm 1. This demonstrates how UCB naturally balances exploration of poorly-estimated arms with exploitation of known good ones.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Why does UCB1\'s regret bound have a 1/Δ_a factor? What does this imply about performance when arms are very close in quality?',
        a: 'The 1/Δ_a factor reflects that near-optimal arms (small Δ_a) require many pulls to confidently identify them as sub-optimal. The confidence interval for arm a must shrink to width less than Δ_a before the algorithm stops pulling it. The number of pulls needed is O(ln T / Δ_a²). Multiplying by Δ_a (the per-pull regret) gives O(ln T / Δ_a) total regret contribution per arm. When arms are very close (Δ_a ≈ 0), regret can be very large — O(ln T / ε) for any ε > 0. In the limit Δ → 0, the lower bound diverges: any algorithm must explore heavily to distinguish near-equal arms, accumulating large regret. This is the correct behavior: if all arms are nearly identical, exploration is expensive but necessary. In practice this means UCB (and all algorithms) perform poorly when the reward signal is noisy and arms are close — you need either very large T or domain knowledge to resolve this.',
      },
      {
        q: 'You\'re designing an ad serving system with 10 million ads. Describe a practical UCB architecture that is computationally feasible.',
        a: 'Naive UCB is O(10M) per request — infeasible at thousands of QPS. Practical approaches: (1) Two-stage selection: use a fast retrieval stage (ANN on embedding similarity, diversity constraints) to produce a candidate set of K=100 ads per request, then run UCB over K=100. (2) Pre-compute UCB scores: batch update UCB index for all 10M ads every B=1000 rounds. Store scores in a low-latency key-value store (Redis/Bigtable). Retrieve scores for candidate set at serve time. This amortizes compute: 10M updates per batch, O(K) lookup per request. (3) Hierarchical UCB: tree structure over ads (e.g., ad category → advertiser → ad). UCB at each level selects which subtree to descend into. O(log K) per round, updates are local. (4) Tiered exploration: only run UCB exploration for ads with < 1000 impressions; for high-impression ads, use pure exploitation. Caps the tail of cold-start exploration cost. (5) Shared parameters (LinUCB): represent ad rewards as linear function of ad features, reducing the bandit problem to parameter estimation over a lower-dimensional space rather than K separate mean estimates.',
      },
      {
        q: 'What is the difference between instance-optimal regret (UCB1) and minimax-optimal regret (MOSS)? When does each matter?',
        a: 'Instance-optimal regret minimizes E[R_T] for each specific bandit instance (each specific set of arm means). UCB1 achieves Σ_a O(ln T / Δ_a) — good when gaps Δ_a are large, but can be worse than Θ(√(KT)) when all gaps are small (Δ ≈ 1/√T). Minimax-optimal regret minimizes the worst-case E[R_T] over all possible bandit instances with K arms. MOSS achieves O(√(KT)) in the worst case — this is tight because the minimax lower bound is Ω(√(KT)). When to use each: instance-optimal (UCB1, KL-UCB) is preferable when you have prior knowledge that arm gaps are not too small — typical for well-separated ad creative performance or clinical treatments with distinct efficacy profiles. Minimax-optimal (MOSS) is preferable when you have no instance information and need robustness guarantees — competitive analysis or regulatory settings where worst-case performance matters. In practice, Thompson Sampling often outperforms both by adapting implicitly to instance difficulty.',
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `UCB operationalises "optimism in the face of uncertainty" — it always selects the arm whose true mean could plausibly be highest given what has been observed. The key formula insight is that the confidence bonus √(2 ln t / N_a) shrinks as an arm accumulates data, so exploration is focused on genuinely uncertain arms rather than all arms uniformly. Understanding the 1/Δ_a regret factor — near-optimal arms are hard to eliminate and require O(log T / Δ_a) pulls each — is what separates a deep understanding from surface familiarity.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'thompson_sampling',
    title: 'Thompson Sampling',
    subtitle: 'Bayesian posterior sampling, conjugate models, empirical performance, top-2 TS',
    difficulty: 'intermediate',
    estimatedMin: 50,
    tags: ['Thompson Sampling', 'Bayesian', 'posterior', 'Beta-Binomial', 'conjugate'],
    summary: `Thompson Sampling (TS) is a Bayesian bandit algorithm: maintain a posterior distribution over each arm's reward parameter, sample one value per arm from each posterior, and pull the arm with the highest sample. As data accumulates, posteriors concentrate near the true means, making exploitative choices increasingly likely while maintaining calibrated exploration proportional to posterior uncertainty. TS achieves the same Lai-Robbins optimal O(log T) regret as UCB but typically outperforms UCB empirically at finite horizons because posterior sampling more efficiently aggregates information than confidence intervals, especially in the low-data regime where priors provide meaningful regularization.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'Beta-Binomial conjugate: for Bernoulli rewards (clicks), maintain Beta(α_a, β_a) posterior per arm. Update: on reward r ∈ {0,1} from arm a, α_a += r, β_a += 1−r. Initial prior Beta(1,1) = Uniform[0,1] (uninformative). To act: sample θ_a ~ Beta(α_a, β_a) for each arm, pull argmax_a θ_a.',
      'Gaussian Thompson Sampling: for real-valued rewards r_a ~ N(μ_a, σ²) with known σ², the conjugate prior is N(μ_0, τ_0²). Posterior after N_a pulls with sample mean x̄_a: μ_{a,post} ~ N((μ_0/τ_0² + N_a·x̄_a/σ²) / (1/τ_0² + N_a/σ²), 1/(1/τ_0² + N_a/σ²)). Unknown variance requires Normal-InverseGamma conjugate.',
      'Why TS often beats UCB empirically: (1) Posterior sampling naturally adapts exploration to the full shape of uncertainty, not just the confidence interval width. (2) Prior information (Beta(α_0, β_0) with α_0 > 1) provides meaningful regularization early on — more efficient than UCB\'s worst-case confidence bounds. (3) TS exploration is proportional to the probability that arm a is optimal, P(θ_a = max_j θ_j) — arms near-optimal get near-optimal exploration rates, a more efficient allocation than UCB\'s uniform confidence bonus.',
      'Top-2 Thompson Sampling (TTTS): a variant for pure exploration (best arm identification, not regret minimization). In each round: (1) Sample θ_a ~ posterior for all arms. (2) With probability β, pull the best sample argmax θ_a. (3) With probability 1−β, resample until a different arm is best, pull it. TTTS achieves sample complexity matching the optimal fixed-budget algorithm for best arm identification.',
      'Particle filter approximation: when rewards are from a complex non-conjugate family (e.g., neural reward model), maintain a particle approximation of the posterior. Each particle is a parameter vector θ^(k). Update via sequential importance resampling. This enables TS for contextual bandits with neural networks (NeuralTS).',
      'Practical: A/B/n tests as multi-armed bandits. Classical A/B testing runs fixed-allocation designs and applies a frequentist hypothesis test at the end. TS reframes this as a sequential bandit: continuously update posteriors, make allocation decisions each round. Advantage: adapts allocation toward better variants during the experiment. Risk: inflated Type-I error if you use standard frequentist stopping rules on TS data — must use Bayesian stopping criteria or sequential hypothesis testing (e.g., always-valid p-values).',
      'Regret analysis: TS achieves E[R_T] = O(√(KT ln K)) in the frequentist worst case (Russo & Van Roy 2014) and matches the Lai-Robbins lower bound asymptotically for specific parametric families (Kaufmann et al. 2012 for Bernoulli). The theoretical guarantees are somewhat harder to prove than UCB because TS is randomized and the analysis requires bounding the probability that TS "misses" the optimal arm.',
      'Computational cost: at each round, TS requires one sample from each posterior. For Beta distributions this is O(K) with fast sampling. For Gaussian, also O(K). For large K (millions of ads), use hierarchical TS or approximate posteriors. For neural posteriors, approximate sampling via dropout (MC Dropout) or ensembles (deep ensembles as TS).',
      'Failure mode: prior misspecification. If Beta(1,1) prior is used but the true CTR is 0.001 (rare clicks), the prior puts 50% probability mass above 0.5 — massive over-exploration initially. Better: informative prior Beta(α_0, β_0) calibrated from historical data. But mis-calibrated priors can also cause under-exploration: Beta(100, 9900) for 1% CTR prior; new arms need many pulls to move the posterior significantly.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: 'Walk through one step of Beta-Binomial Thompson Sampling for 3 ads with posteriors Beta(10,90), Beta(5,45), Beta(1,1). What are the likely samples and which arm gets pulled?',
        a: 'The three arms have empirical means: arm 1 = 10/100 = 0.10, arm 2 = 5/50 = 0.10, arm 3 = 1/2 = 0.50. But the posteriors differ in concentration: arm 1 (100 observations) is very concentrated near 0.10; arm 3 (2 observations) is nearly uniform. Sampling: θ_1 ~ Beta(10,90) ≈ concentrated near 0.10, likely 0.07–0.14. θ_2 ~ Beta(5,45) ≈ concentrated near 0.10, wider, likely 0.05–0.18. θ_3 ~ Beta(1,1) ~ Uniform[0,1], likely anywhere from 0 to 1. P(arm 3 pulled) ≈ P(θ_3 > max(θ_1, θ_2)) — since θ_3 is uniform and arms 1,2 are concentrated near 0.10, arm 3 gets pulled roughly 50% of the time (any θ_3 > ~0.15 wins). This is the right behavior: arm 3 is highly uncertain and could be much better than 0.10 — TS explores it aggressively. After a pull: if r=1, posterior → Beta(2,1); if r=0, posterior → Beta(1,2). Over 50 pulls, Beta(1,1) concentrates near the true mean and exploration decreases proportionally.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Your team is running an A/B/C test (3 variants) using Thompson Sampling. After 1000 rounds, the posterior probability that variant B is best is 92%. A PM wants to stop and declare B the winner. What questions do you ask before agreeing?',
        a: 'Key questions: (1) What stopping criterion was pre-specified? "P(best) > 92%" was likely not pre-specified, raising concerns about optional stopping bias — you may have stopped at the first crossing of an arbitrary threshold. Bayesian optional stopping is valid under proper Bayesian decision theory but requires a pre-registered criterion. (2) What is the practical significance? P(B is best) = 92% does not tell you the expected loss from choosing B. Compute the Expected Regret of Stopping: E[Δ(B)] = P(B not best) · E[μ_best − μ_B | B not best]. If this is < 0.001 CTR, stopping is justified; if > 0.01, wait. (3) Are rewards stationary? TS posteriors reflect the mixture of historical reward distributions. If rewards shifted during the experiment, the posterior conflates different populations. (4) Sample size: 1000 rounds across 3 arms means ~333 per arm. With Bernoulli(0.1) rewards, standard deviation of click estimate is 0.3/√333 ≈ 0.016. Is the effect size detectable? (5) External validity: is the traffic during this experiment representative? Time-of-day, day-of-week effects in 1000 rounds may not average out.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Explain why Thompson Sampling exploration is proportional to P(arm a is optimal) and why this is more efficient than UCB\'s exploration.',
        a: 'TS pulls arm a with probability P(θ_a = max_j θ_j | observations) — exactly the probability that arm a is currently believed optimal. In the stochastic setting, the optimal pull allocation for minimizing regret is roughly: spend O(ln T / Δ_a²) pulls on arm a. TS naturally approximates this because: as arm a accumulates observations, its posterior concentrates; the probability that a random sample from its posterior beats all other arms\' samples shrinks (if a is sub-optimal) or grows (if a is optimal). This is called "probability matching" and has the property that it focuses exploration precisely on arms that have meaningful probability of being optimal. UCB in contrast applies a fixed confidence bonus √(2 ln t / N_a) regardless of the arm\'s current probability of being optimal. For an arm with 1000 pulls and empirical mean 0.1 when the best arm has mean 0.9, UCB still gives it exploration credit; TS assigns it near-zero pull probability because no sample from its concentrated posterior will beat 0.9. In practice this makes TS faster to "give up" on clearly inferior arms.',
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `Thompson Sampling exploration is proportional to P(arm a is optimal) — arms that cannot plausibly be best receive negligible exploration budget as their posteriors concentrate. This "probability matching" property is why TS is more efficient than UCB at finite horizons: it gives up on clearly inferior arms faster, whereas UCB continues to apply a fixed confidence bonus regardless of how implausible it is that the arm is optimal. In production, the critical practical issue is prior misspecification — a Beta(1,1) prior on an arm with true CTR of 0.001 wastes enormous exploration budget early on.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'contextual_bandits',
    title: 'Contextual Bandits',
    subtitle: 'Context-dependent rewards, LinUCB, LinTS, offline evaluation, NeuralUCB',
    difficulty: 'intermediate',
    estimatedMin: 55,
    tags: ['contextual bandit', 'LinUCB', 'LinTS', 'exploration', 'function approximation'],
    summary: `Contextual bandits extend the multi-armed bandit by providing a context vector at each round — features describing the user, item, and situation — and the reward depends on both the arm and the context. This subsumes classical A/B testing (context is the user) and personalised recommendation (different users prefer different items). The core algorithmic challenge is function approximation: estimating reward(arm, context) well enough to make good decisions while maintaining calibrated uncertainty for exploration. Linear models (LinUCB, LinTS) admit exact uncertainty quantification; neural models require approximations (NeuralUCB, NeuralTS) but capture non-linear interactions.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'Contextual bandit formulation: at round t, observe context x_t ∈ R^d, choose arm a_t ∈ {1,...,K}, observe reward r_t = f(x_t, a_t) + noise. Goal: minimize Σ_t [f(x_t, a*_t) − f(x_t, a_t)] where a*_t = argmax_a f(x_t, a). Context subsumes the round index — every round is potentially different.',
      'Subsumes classical A/B testing: in a standard A/B test, context is the user features and "arms" are variants. A contextual bandit learns which variant is better for which user — essentially learning heterogeneous treatment effects while running the experiment. Classical A/B tests throw away context during allocation; contextual bandits use it.',
      'LinUCB (linear UCB): assumes reward r_t = θ_a^T x_t + ε_t where θ_a is an arm-specific parameter vector. Estimates θ_a via ridge regression over observed (context, reward) pairs for arm a. Constructs a confidence ellipsoid around θ̂_a. UCB for arm a = θ̂_a^T x_t + α√(x_t^T A_a^{-1} x_t) where A_a = X_a^T X_a + λI is the regularized Gram matrix.',
      'LinTS (linear Thompson Sampling): posterior over θ_a is Gaussian with mean θ̂_a and covariance σ²A_a^{-1}. At each round sample θ̃_a ~ N(θ̂_a, σ²A_a^{-1}) and select arm a = argmax_a θ̃_a^T x_t. Same regret bounds as LinUCB but empirically better, especially with informative priors.',
      'Offline policy evaluation (OPE): you have a logged dataset of (context, arm, reward) tuples from a logging policy (e.g., random or the old policy). You want to evaluate a new policy without deploying it. OPE is essential for contextual bandits because online deployment is expensive and risky. Key estimators: importance sampling (IS), doubly robust (DR), direct method (DM). Each has bias-variance tradeoffs (covered in depth in the OPE module).',
      'Challenge of function approximation in contextual bandits: linear models may not capture the true reward function. Moving to non-linear models (trees, neural nets) breaks the exact uncertainty quantification that makes LinUCB/LinTS work. NeuralUCB (Zhou et al. 2020) uses the neural tangent kernel (NTK) to build confidence bounds for infinitely-wide networks. NeuralTS uses a last-layer Thompson Sampling approximation (fix all but last layer, TS on linear head). Both require the network to be sufficiently wide and the rewards to be in the NTK RKHS.',
      'Last-layer LinTS (practical standard): train a neural network to extract features φ(x, a), fix it periodically, run LinTS on the linear head. This is the most common production approach: neural features + linear uncertainty quantification. The risk: the representation φ may not capture relevant uncertainty if the base model is confident in the wrong direction.',
      'Epsilon-greedy for contextual bandits: the simplest baseline — train a reward model on logged data, use it greedily with ε probability of random arm. Surprisingly effective when the reward model is good and the context space is well-covered by historical data. The problem: no principled exploration bonus — exploration is uniform over arms, ignoring context.',
      'Regret bounds for contextual bandits: LinUCB achieves O(d√T ln K) regret where d is the context dimension. NeuralUCB achieves O(√(T·complexity of reward function)) under NTK assumptions. In both cases, exploration cost scales with the complexity of the reward model — higher-dimensional or more complex reward functions require more exploration.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: 'How does a contextual bandit differ from a supervised learning model + greedy selection? What goes wrong with the greedy approach?',
        a: 'A supervised model trained on logged data + greedy selection (direct method / DM) selects the arm with highest predicted reward each round. This fails in several ways: (1) Exploration bias: greedy never explores arms the logging policy undersampled, so the reward model remains uncertain about them forever. If arm A was rarely shown in logs, the model\'s prediction for arm A is poorly calibrated, and greedy may perpetually avoid it or over-trust it. (2) Distributional shift: the deployment distribution differs from the training distribution as the policy changes. Reward model accuracy degrades. (3) No exploration bonus: the greedy policy doesn\'t quantify uncertainty. Arms that were shown only once in logs but happened to have a high reward get high predicted reward with no uncertainty penalty — pure exploitation of noise. A contextual bandit (LinUCB/LinTS) adds uncertainty-based exploration: arms with high uncertainty get an exploration bonus, ensuring the model improves on under-explored regions. The key theoretical difference: DM has no regret guarantee; LinUCB has O(d√T) regret guarantee under realizability.',
      },
      {
        q: 'You are building a contextual bandit for mobile push notification personalization. Context = 50-dim user features. K=20 notification types. How do you choose between LinUCB (disjoint), LinUCB (hybrid), and NeuralTS?',
        a: 'Three choices: (1) LinUCB disjoint — separate θ_a ∈ R^d for each arm. Total parameters = K·d = 1000. Arms with few observations have high uncertainty. Good when arms have very different response patterns to the same user features. Problem: no information sharing across arms. (2) LinUCB hybrid — shared + arm-specific parameters: reward = θ_shared^T z_t + θ_a^T x_t. Shared component captures common user-feature relationships; arm-specific captures arm differences. Better sample efficiency when arm responses share structure. (3) NeuralTS — neural feature extractor φ(x), LinTS on linear head. Captures non-linear interactions between user features and notification type. Requires substantial historical data (>>K·d observations) to train a stable neural representation. For 20 arms and 50 features: start with LinUCB hybrid. If you have < 10,000 observations per arm, linear is more reliable. Monitor reward model calibration; switch to NeuralTS with frozen representation when you have >100K total observations and see evidence of non-linear interactions (e.g., cross-validation reward prediction error plateaus for linear model).',
      },
      {
        q: 'Describe a contextual bandit deployment pipeline for news article recommendation. What are the main engineering challenges?',
        a: 'Pipeline: (1) Context construction: at request time, assemble user features (demographics, history embedding, recency signals), article features (topic, freshness, popularity), cross features (topic-user affinity). Latency budget: < 5ms. (2) Candidate generation: retrieve top-M=100 candidate articles from ANN index. (3) Contextual bandit scoring: for each candidate, compute UCB score = θ̂_a^T x + α√(x^T A^{-1} x). Arm = article, x = concatenated user-article features. (4) Serve: return top-ranked article. (5) Feedback loop: log (context, arm, reward=click) asynchronously. Update A_a and b_a via Sherman-Morrison online update (O(d²) per observation). Engineering challenges: (a) Delayed feedback — user may click 10 seconds later; must queue (context, arm) pairs and match with eventual reward. (b) Covariate shift — user distribution changes over time; periodically retrain or use discounted regression. (c) Matrix inversion — A_a^{-1} must be updated online (Sherman-Morrison) and is d×d; d=50 is manageable, d=500 requires approximations (diagonal approx, low-rank updates). (d) Cold start — new articles have zero arm-specific observations; use shared model only until N_a > d. (e) Evaluation without deployment — use OPE (doubly robust) on held-out logged data before each policy change.',
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `Contextual bandits subsume standard A/B testing — they learn which arm is best for which user, not just which arm is best on average. The critical failure mode of the naive alternative (supervised model + greedy selection) is exploration bias: arms underrepresented in the logging policy have poorly calibrated reward estimates and are either permanently avoided or over-trusted. LinUCB's uncertainty bonus √(x^T A^{-1} x) is the key mechanism — it is largest for contexts that are far from previously observed data, ensuring exploration is targeted at novel contexts rather than wasted uniformly.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'linucb',
    title: 'LinUCB In Depth',
    subtitle: 'Ridge regression reward model, confidence ellipsoid, disjoint vs hybrid, Sherman-Morrison',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 60,
    tags: ['LinUCB', 'ridge regression', 'confidence ellipsoid', 'Sherman-Morrison', 'disjoint', 'hybrid'],
    summary: `LinUCB formalizes contextual bandit exploration as ridge regression with uncertainty quantification via confidence ellipsoids. The key insight from Li et al. (2010) — the Yahoo! news recommendation paper — is that the ridge regression parameter covariance matrix A^{-1} provides a principled upper confidence bound on the reward: UCB = θ̂^T x + α√(x^T A^{-1} x), where x^T A^{-1} x measures the "statistical distance" of the new context from previously observed contexts. This framework enables exact O(d√T ln K) regret guarantees and supports efficient online updates via Sherman-Morrison rank-1 updates, making it deployable in real-time systems at scale.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'Ridge regression for reward: for arm a with observations {(x_1,r_1),...,(x_n,r_n)}, the ridge estimate is θ̂_a = A_a^{-1} b_a where A_a = Σ_i x_i x_i^T + λI (regularized Gram matrix, λ > 0) and b_a = Σ_i r_i x_i. The λI term ensures A_a is invertible even with few observations and acts as an isotropic prior (Gaussian prior θ ~ N(0, I/λ)).',
      'Confidence ellipsoid: under the linear model r_t = θ_a^T x_t + ε_t with ε_t subgaussian(σ²), the true θ_a lies in the ellipsoid {θ : (θ − θ̂_a)^T A_a (θ − θ̂_a) ≤ β} with high probability (1 − δ). The width of this ellipsoid in direction x is √(x^T A_a^{-1} x). UCB = max_{θ in ellipsoid} θ^T x = θ̂_a^T x + α√(x^T A_a^{-1} x).',
      'Confidence parameter α: theory sets α = σ√(d ln((T λ + T)/δ)) + √λ ≈ O(σ√(d ln T)). In practice, α is tuned as a hyperparameter — too large → over-exploration (high regret from pulling uncertain arms), too small → under-exploration (misses better arms). The typical production heuristic: α ∈ [0.1, 1.0] tuned on held-out OPE metrics.',
      'Disjoint model: separate (A_a, b_a, θ̂_a) per arm. No information sharing across arms. Works best when arm response patterns are genuinely distinct. Parameter count: K × d × d for A_a (symmetric, so K × d(d+1)/2). With K=100, d=50, this is 100 × 1275 = 127,500 floats — feasible.',
      'Hybrid model: reward = β^T z_t + θ_a^T x_t where z_t = shared features (e.g., user-only), x_t = arm-specific features (e.g., user-item interaction). β is shared across all arms, θ_a is arm-specific. Regression solution requires maintaining cross-arm statistics (Ã matrix for shared parameters). More sample-efficient but harder to implement — requires solving a joint system across arms at each update.',
      'Sherman-Morrison online update: given A_a = X^T X + λI, after one new observation (x, r): A_a_new = A_a + x x^T, b_a_new = b_a + r x. Naively, recomputing A_a^{-1} costs O(d³). Sherman-Morrison: (A + x x^T)^{-1} = A^{-1} − (A^{-1} x x^T A^{-1}) / (1 + x^T A^{-1} x). Cost: O(d²) — two matrix-vector products. This enables real-time updates at millions of QPS.',
      'Failure mode — reward model misspecification: if true reward is f(x) = sin(x^T θ) (non-linear), the linear model is misspecified. The confidence ellipsoid no longer contains the true parameter — the algorithm may be systematically over-confident in the wrong direction, leading to under-exploration or persistent arm selection errors. Detection: monitor reward model residuals; large systematic residuals indicate misspecification. Fix: feature engineering (polynomial features, interaction terms) or switch to neural model.',
      'Failure mode — covariate shift: the distribution of contexts at deployment time differs from historical training data. A_a was built on historical contexts; x^T A_a^{-1} x may be small for new context distributions (in-distribution for historical, out-of-distribution for new users). The UCB is under-calibrated — the algorithm is over-confident for contexts far from the training distribution. Fix: periodic A_a reset with a forgetting factor (A_a ← γ A_a + x x^T) or use only recent observations.',
      'Regret bound: LinUCB with disjoint model achieves E[R_T] = O(d√T ln(KT/δ)) with probability 1−δ. This is worse than pure MAB O(√(KT)) by a factor of √d — the context learning cost. With K arms and d features, you effectively have K·d parameters to learn, but shared context structure reduces this to d. The bound is tight: contextual bandits are fundamentally harder than MAB by the feature dimension factor.',
      'LinUCB in Yahoo! news (Li et al. 2010): evaluated on 36M event log from Yahoo! front page. K=~20 articles at any time (refreshed), d=6 features (user clusters). Disjoint LinUCB with α tuned on 10% validation data outperformed random policy by 12.5% CTR lift. Key finding: α=0.2 was optimal (much less exploration than theory suggests), because the reward model was already well-specified (linear structure was a good approximation for user-article affinities).',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: 'Derive the LinUCB index from first principles. Why is √(x^T A^{-1} x) the right uncertainty measure?',
        a: 'Start with ridge regression: θ̂ = (X^T X + λI)^{-1} X^T r = A^{-1} b. The ridge estimator has posterior distribution (under Gaussian likelihood and prior θ ~ N(0, I/λ)): θ | data ~ N(θ̂, σ² A^{-1}). The predictive mean for a new context x is θ̂^T x with variance σ² x^T A^{-1} x. The 95th percentile upper confidence bound for the expected reward θ^T x is: UCB(x) = θ̂^T x + α σ √(x^T A^{-1} x). Why √(x^T A^{-1} x)? It is the standard deviation of the predicted reward for context x under the posterior. Geometrically: A = X^T X + λI measures how much information the observed contexts X contain about directions in R^d. A^{-1} has large eigenvalues in directions orthogonal to observed data — high posterior variance there. x^T A^{-1} x projects x onto these high-variance directions; if x aligns with data-sparse directions, the UCB is large (high uncertainty → exploration). If x aligns with data-rich directions, UCB is small (low uncertainty → exploitation). This is exactly the right exploration signal: explore when the current context is novel relative to historical observations.',
      },
      {
        q: 'You\'re implementing LinUCB with d=100 feature dimensions and K=50 arms. The system receives 10,000 requests/second. Describe the computational challenges and how you address them.',
        a: 'Per-request compute: (1) For each candidate arm a, compute UCB_a = θ̂_a^T x + α√(x^T A_a^{-1} x). x^T A_a^{-1} x requires: compute v = A_a^{-1} x (matrix-vector product, O(d²) = 10,000 ops), then v^T x (dot product, O(d) = 100 ops). For K=50 arms: 50 × 10,100 ≈ 505,000 FLOPs per request. At 10,000 QPS: 5 × 10^9 FLOPs/second — around 5 GFLOPS, feasible on a single modern server core. (2) Memory: store A_a^{-1} (not A_a) directly — d×d float32 per arm = 100×100×4 bytes = 40KB per arm, 2MB total for 50 arms. Fine. Per-observation update: Sherman-Morrison: v = A_a^{-1} x, A_a^{-1}_new = A_a^{-1} − v v^T / (1 + x^T v). Cost: O(d²) = 10,000 ops. b_a update: O(d). At 10,000 QPS with full feedback rate, update load is 10,000 × 10,000 = 10^8 ops/sec — feasible on one core. Practical issues: (a) Concurrent writes to A_a^{-1} require locking or lock-free updates (Hogwild-style, accept slight staleness). (b) Delayed feedback: buffer (x, r) pairs and apply updates asynchronously. (c) Matrix numerical stability: A_a^{-1} can accumulate floating point errors; periodically recompute from scratch via Cholesky factorization.',
      },
      {
        q: 'In the Yahoo! news experiment, α=0.2 was optimal, far below the theoretically motivated α=O(√(d ln T)). What does this imply about the theory-practice gap in LinUCB?',
        a: 'Theory sets α = O(σ√(d ln T)) to guarantee that the true θ lies in the confidence ellipsoid with high probability. With d=6, T=36M, σ≈0.5, this gives α ≈ 0.5 × √(6 × 17.4) ≈ 5.1. The optimal empirical α=0.2 is ~25× smaller. What this implies: (1) The theoretical α is extremely conservative — it guarantees coverage over all possible θ and all possible realizations, including worst-case noise sequences. The actual error in θ̂ is much smaller than the theoretical bound suggests. (2) Real reward noise σ may be much smaller than assumed. (3) The feature representation may have low effective dimensionality (most arms are explained by 1-2 features), so the effective d is smaller than 6. (4) Over-exploration is costly: high α means many pulls on uncertain arms that are actually sub-optimal; the empirical optimum trades off the theoretical regret bound (which assumes worst case) for actual finite-T performance. Practical implication: always tune α on held-out OPE data; do not use the theoretical value directly. The theory tells you the right functional form (UCB = mean + α√(x^T A^{-1} x)) but not the constant.',
      },
      {
        q: 'How does LinUCB handle the cold-start problem for a completely new arm that has never been shown to any user?',
        a: 'For a new arm a with zero observations: A_a = λI (just the regularization term, no data). θ̂_a = A_a^{-1} b_a = 0 (no prior reward signal, or use informative prior). UCB_a(x) = θ̂_a^T x + α√(x^T (λI)^{-1} x) = α/√λ · ||x||. The confidence bonus is α/√λ · ||x||_2 — proportional to the feature norm of the new context. If features are normalized (||x||=1), every new arm starts with UCB = α/√λ regardless of context. This is higher than established arms unless they have very high empirical means. So LinUCB automatically gives new arms high UCB scores — a natural cold-start exploration bonus. However: the bonus is uniform across contexts for the new arm (since θ̂_a = 0, there\'s no personalization). In practice, you can seed A_a and b_a with a prior θ̂_prior estimated from: (a) content features of the new arm (e.g., topic-level CTR historical average), (b) similar arms\' parameters (content-based warm start), (c) a global θ̂_global trained on all arms. This biases the initial exploration toward contexts where similar arms have performed well.',
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `The geometric insight behind LinUCB is what matters: x^T A^{-1} x measures how far the current context x is from the span of previously observed contexts, so the exploration bonus is largest exactly when the algorithm is in unfamiliar territory. The Yahoo! news finding that optimal α was 25× smaller than theoretical predictions is a reminder that worst-case theoretical constants are not production constants — always tune α on held-out OPE data, and use Sherman-Morrison for O(d²) online updates rather than O(d³) full matrix inversions.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'off_policy_evaluation',
    title: 'Off-Policy Evaluation for Bandits',
    subtitle: 'Importance sampling, doubly robust estimator, SNIPS, variance control, distribution shift',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 65,
    tags: ['OPE', 'importance sampling', 'doubly robust', 'SNIPS', 'IPW', 'policy evaluation'],
    summary: `Off-policy evaluation (OPE) answers the question: given logged data from a behavior policy π_b (e.g., the current recommendation system), how well would a new target policy π_e perform if deployed? This is critical for bandits and recommendation systems because online A/B testing every policy change is expensive and risky. The core challenge is distribution shift: the behavior policy chose arms based on its own logic, so the observed (context, arm, reward) distribution reflects π_b, not π_e. Inverse propensity weighting corrects for this but at the cost of high variance when propensities are small; the doubly robust estimator combines a direct reward model with importance weighting to achieve variance reduction while maintaining consistency.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'Direct method (DM): train a reward model r̂(x, a) on logged data, evaluate policy as V̂_DM(π_e) = Σ_t Σ_a π_e(a|x_t) · r̂(x_t, a). Bias: r̂ may be wrong in regions where π_e takes actions that π_b never explored. Variance: low — no importance weights. When to use: r̂ is very accurate and actions of π_e are well-covered by π_b.',
      'Importance Sampling (IS) estimator: V̂_IS(π_e) = (1/T) Σ_t w_t · r_t where w_t = π_e(a_t | x_t) / π_b(a_t | x_t) is the importance weight. Unbiased: E[V̂_IS] = V(π_e). Variance: Var(w · r) can be enormous when π_e concentrates on actions π_b rarely took (small propensity denominator).',
      'SNIPS (Self-Normalized Importance Sampling): V̂_SNIPS = Σ_t w_t r_t / Σ_t w_t. Normalizes by the sum of weights to reduce variance. Biased but consistent; variance often much lower than IS. Practical default when propensities vary significantly across rounds.',
      'Doubly Robust (DR) estimator: V̂_DR = (1/T) Σ_t [r̂(x_t, a_t) + w_t (r_t − r̂(x_t, a_t))]. Combines DM (first term) with IS correction for the residual (second term). Properties: (1) If r̂ is correct, DR = DM (low variance). (2) If π_b is correctly specified (propensities known), DR = IS (unbiased). (3) "Doubly robust" — consistent if either the reward model OR the propensity model is correct, not necessarily both.',
      'Variance explosion with small propensities: if π_b assigned probability 0.01 to action a but π_e assigns 0.9, the weight w = 90. With r ∈ [0,1], the contribution w·r can be 90× the reward. With high-variance weights, V̂_IS can have variance proportional to (max_w)² — practically useless with clipping or trimming required.',
      'Propensity clipping: cap weights at W_max (e.g., W_max = 20). Reduces variance at the cost of bias. The bias is bounded by the probability mass on clipped weights × reward range. Tuning W_max: monitor the effective sample size N_eff = (Σ w_t)² / Σ w_t² — should be > 10% of T for reliable estimates.',
      'Why OPE is hard — partial feedback: we observe only r(a_t) — the reward for the taken action. We do NOT observe r(a) for a ≠ a_t. This is the fundamental difference from supervised learning, where ground truth labels exist for all classes. OPE must estimate counterfactual rewards (what would have happened if π_e had chosen differently) from this partial information.',
      'Distribution shift at horizon: if π_b trained a sequential policy (e.g., a previously deployed bandit), the log data is NOT i.i.d. — context distribution at time t may depend on actions taken at times 1,...,t-1. Standard OPE estimators assume i.i.d. rounds; sequential dependencies require marginal importance weights or marginalized importance sampling (MIS).',
      'Netflix and Spotify application: Netflix evaluates new ranking policies via DR estimator on logged impression data before A/B testing. Spotify uses OPE to estimate playlist CTR for new recommendation policies. Key insight: OPE enables rapid iteration — evaluate 100 policies offline in the time it takes to A/B test 1. Typical OPE pipeline: (1) collect logs from π_b with propensity logging, (2) train reward model r̂ on logs, (3) compute V̂_DR for each candidate π_e, (4) promote top candidates to online A/B test.',
      'Convergence rates: IS estimator has variance O(1/(T·N_eff)) where N_eff is effective sample size. DR estimator achieves the semiparametric efficiency bound: variance = O((V(π_e)(1 − V(π_e)) + E[σ²_reward])/T) when both nuisance models are well-estimated — lower variance than IS by a factor of the average residual variance over the importance weight variance.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: 'You have 1M logged impressions from a random policy (uniform over K=10 arms) and want to evaluate a new deterministic policy that always chooses arm 3. Compute the importance weights and analyze the variance.',
        a: 'π_b(a|x) = 1/10 = 0.1 for all arms (uniform logging). π_e(a|x) = 1 if a=arm3, else 0 (deterministic). Importance weight for round t: w_t = π_e(a_t|x_t) / π_b(a_t|x_t). If a_t = arm3: w_t = 1/0.1 = 10. If a_t ≠ arm3: w_t = 0/0.1 = 0. IS estimate: V̂_IS = (Σ_t w_t r_t) / T = 10 × (Σ_{t: a_t=arm3} r_t) / T = 10 × (N_3 / T) × r̄_3 where N_3 ≈ T/10 is the number of arm3 pulls and r̄_3 is the mean reward on arm3. So V̂_IS ≈ r̄_3 — the simple empirical mean reward of arm3. Variance analysis: w_t ∈ {0, 10}, Var(w_t r_t) ≤ E[w_t² r_t²] ≤ 100 × E[r_t² 1(a_t=arm3)] = 100 × (1/10) × E[r²|arm3] = 10 × E[r²|arm3]. This is manageable because the propensities don\'t get very small relative to π_e (max weight = 10). The effective sample size N_eff = T × P(a_t=arm3) = T/10 = 100,000 — good. In this case OPE is easy because π_e is deterministic and the logging policy has full support (all arms logged with 1/K propensity).',
      },
      {
        q: 'Why is the doubly robust estimator called "doubly robust"? Construct a simple example where one model is wrong but DR is still consistent.',
        a: '"Doubly robust" means the estimator is consistent if either (but not necessarily both) the reward model r̂ or the propensity model π_b is correctly specified. V̂_DR = (1/T) Σ_t [r̂(x_t,a_t) + w_t(r_t − r̂(x_t,a_t))]. Case 1: propensities are correct (w_t = π_e/π_b correctly), reward model r̂ is wrong. The term w_t(r_t − r̂) is an importance-weighted correction for the bias in r̂. In expectation: E[w_t(r_t − r̂)] = V(π_e) − E_{π_b}[w_t r̂(x_t,a_t)] = V(π_e) − V̂_DM(π_e). So DR → DM + (V(π_e) − DM) = V(π_e). Case 2: reward model r̂ is correct (r̂(x,a) = E[r|x,a]), propensity model is wrong. DR = V̂_DM + (1/T)Σ w_t(r_t − r̂). But if r̂ is correct, r_t − r̂(x_t,a_t) is mean-zero noise regardless of w_t, so the IS correction term averages to zero. DR → DM = V(π_e). Example: logged data has 3 rounds. True: V(π_e)=0.5. Scenario: r̂ predicts 0.8 everywhere (wrong). Propensities are correctly 0.5. w_t=1 (propensities match exactly). DR = 0.8 + 1×(r_t−0.8) averaged = r̄ = 0.5. Correct despite wrong r̂.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Spotify wants to evaluate 50 new playlist ranking policies before A/B testing. Describe the full OPE pipeline and the key failure modes you need to monitor.',
        a: 'Pipeline: (1) Logging: deploy current policy π_b with propensity logging — for each impression, log (user_context x_t, item_ranked_first a_t, propensity π_b(a_t|x_t), click_reward r_t). Log propensities carefully — if π_b is a neural ranker with softmax, propensities are the softmax probabilities. (2) Reward model: train r̂(x, a) on logged (x, a, r) tuples. Use cross-validation to estimate r̂ accuracy; monitor calibration (predicted CTR vs actual CTR per decile). (3) OPE computation: for each of 50 candidate policies π_e, compute V̂_DR(π_e) = mean of [r̂(x_t,a_t) + w_t(r_t − r̂(x_t,a_t))]. Report mean ± standard error (bootstrap). (4) Ranking: sort 50 policies by V̂_DR. Promote top 3-5 to online A/B test. Failure modes to monitor: (a) Propensity degeneracy — check distribution of w_t. If P(w_t > 50) > 1%, clip and flag. Compute N_eff = (Σw)²/(Σw²); if < 5% of T, OPE is unreliable. (b) Reward model underfit — if r̂ RMSE > 0.2 on held-out data, DM component is biased; DR variance will be high because residuals are large. (c) Support violation — if π_e assigns probability to (x,a) pairs that π_b never logged, the IS weight is undefined (0/0). These contexts cannot be evaluated — must either exclude or bound. (d) Temporal correlation — if π_b is itself a bandit that adapted over time, early log entries reflect a different policy than later entries. Use temporal splits carefully. (e) OPE-to-online gap — monitor how well OPE estimates predict actual A/B test outcomes. If the rank correlation OPE vs A/B is < 0.7, investigate propensity or model issues.',
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `The doubly robust estimator is the production standard for OPE because it is consistent if either the reward model or the propensity model is correctly specified — giving two chances to be right. The critical operational detail is always logging propensities at serve time (not reconstructing them later), because propensity reconstruction errors break all IS-based estimators. The effective sample size N_eff = (Σw)²/(Σw²) must be monitored continuously: if it falls below 5-10% of the logged sample size, the OPE estimate is dominated by a handful of high-weight observations and is unreliable regardless of sample volume.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'bandits_in_recsys',
    title: 'Bandits in Recommendation Systems',
    subtitle: 'Cold start, exploration bonus in ranking, batched bandits, delayed feedback, cascaded exploration',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 65,
    tags: ['recommendation', 'cold start', 'exploration', 'delayed feedback', 'batched bandits', 'ranking'],
    summary: `Recommendation systems are bandit problems at scale: billions of users, millions of items, and only partial feedback (a user sees one ranking, clicks at most a few items). The naive approach — deploy a pure exploitation recommender — suffers from filter bubbles, cold-start failure, and inability to discover serendipitous preferences. Integrating bandit exploration into ranking requires confronting engineering realities that pure theory ignores: updates are batched (not per-interaction), feedback is delayed (clicks arrive seconds to days after impressions), and exploration must be calibrated to not destroy short-term CTR metrics while building long-term knowledge.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'Cold start as exploration: new items have no historical engagement data. A pure collaborative filtering model scores them near 0 (regression to the mean) and they are never shown — a self-fulfilling prophecy. Bandit solution: assign new items a forced exploration budget (e.g., 1000 impressions) with UCB-style uncertainty bonus. Decay the bonus as impressions accumulate.',
      'Exploration bonus in ranking: integrate bandit exploration into the ranking score: score(item) = predicted_reward(item) + α · uncertainty(item). Uncertainty can be: (1) content-based: new item → high uncertainty regardless of user. (2) user-item: item shown to this user type rarely → high uncertainty for this user type. (3) model-based: variance of ensemble predictions. α is tuned to balance CTR (exploitation) vs exploration efficiency.',
      'Explore-exploit tradeoff at multiple levels: (1) Item level: which item to recommend. (2) User level: for new users with sparse history, explore their preferences (show diverse items, not just popular ones). (3) Context level: new contexts (new device type, new time-of-day pattern) require exploration of how preferences differ.',
      'Delayed feedback: in recommendation, clicks (or streams, purchases) are observed seconds to hours after the recommendation event. Standard bandit theory assumes immediate feedback. Solutions: (1) Optimistic reward imputation — assign unobserved rewards an optimistic value (e.g., 1) until confirmed. (2) Delayed update — buffer (context, arm) pairs and update when reward arrives. Risk: stale statistics during the delay window. (3) Reward shaping — use proxy rewards (scroll time, hover) as fast surrogates for slow rewards (purchase, subscription).',
      'Batched bandits: real production systems update models in batches (hourly, daily), not per-interaction. Classical bandit theory assumes one update per round. In batched settings: (1) All rounds in a batch use the same policy parameters (no within-batch exploration adaptation). (2) Batch UCB: compute UCB at the start of the batch using statistics from all previous batches; the entire batch exploits/explores based on this fixed policy. Regret degrades by factor O(√batch_size) vs online updates — acceptable for large T.',
      'Cascaded exploration: ranking exposes a ranked list [item_1, item_2, ..., item_L]. The user scans top-to-bottom and clicks the first satisfying item (cascade model). This creates a cascade bandit problem: higher positions have much higher click probability (position bias); exploration of lower-ranked items is masked by cascade stopping. Solution: occasionally promote uncertain items to high positions ("exploration slots") and attribute click differences to the position swap. Must use position-aware reward models.',
      'Blend bandits with neural ranker: the production stack is typically: (a) Retrieval: ANN to fetch top-K=500 candidates. (b) Neural ranker: deep model predicts P(click|user, item, context). (c) Bandit layer: adjust neural ranker scores with exploration bonus. Key tension: neural ranker is retrained periodically on logged data — if the bandit layer causes distribution shift (items shown ≠ neural ranker predictions), the next retraining iteration has selection bias. Fix: (1) Train neural ranker on logged data with importance weighting (DR estimator). (2) Constrain exploration bonus to be small relative to ranker score (α ≤ 0.1 × score_range).',
      'Evaluation: online metrics (CTR, revenue) measure exploitation quality. To measure exploration quality: (1) Item discovery rate — fraction of catalog items that receive ≥ N impressions per day. (2) Coverage diversity — entropy of impression distribution over item IDs. (3) Long-term user satisfaction — retention, LTV — exploration should improve these even if it slightly hurts short-term CTR.',
      'Practical: exploration budget. Dedicate X% of traffic to exploration (e.g., 5%). Within that budget, run bandit algorithms. For remaining 95%, run pure exploitation. This makes exploration easy to reason about operationally: "5% traffic is the exploration pool." Exploration budget can be varied by user segment (new users get more exploration), item segment (new items get more exploration), and time of day.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: 'A Netflix-scale system has 50M movies. 10,000 new movies are added each month. Describe a cold-start exploration strategy that doesn\'t crater CTR.',
        a: 'Strategy: (1) Pre-launch content scoring: extract content features (genre, cast, themes, studio) and use a content-based model to predict initial CTR for each new movie based on similar established movies. Initialize UCB prior with this estimate (e.g., Beta(α_0, β_0) where α_0/(α_0+β_0) = content_predicted_CTR). (2) Tiered exploration budget: new movies get a guaranteed 100,000-impression exploration budget over the first 30 days. Allocate impressions via UCB on the content-model-initialized posterior — don\'t waste impressions on new movies for users with no affinity for their genre. (3) Targeted exploration: show new movies primarily to users who: (a) have high engagement with similar established movies (content affinity), (b) explicitly enjoy diverse/new content (high exploration reward from historical behavior), (c) are in high-traffic segments (gives faster feedback). (4) CTR protection: cap exploration traffic at 3% of recommendations per user session. Never show >1 new/exploration item in the top-5 positions. (5) Graduated rollout: after 10K impressions, if CTR > 0.5 × genre average, increase exploration rate. If CTR < 0.1 × genre average, deprioritize (likely niche content). (6) Monitoring: track P(new movie explored within 30 days) and mean impressions-to-first-1000-plays. Alert if new movies take >30 days to get 1000 impressions — indicates cold-start failure.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Your recommendation system updates model parameters once per day (batched bandit). You observe that UCB scores computed at midnight are stale by end of day because popular items have shifted. How do you handle this?',
        a: 'The core problem: UCB scores = f(A_a^{-1}, b_a) computed at midnight reflect observations up to midnight. During the day, new (context, reward) observations accumulate but the UCB policy doesn\'t update. Solutions: (1) More frequent batches: increase update frequency to hourly instead of daily. Requires efficient incremental model updates (Sherman-Morrison for LinUCB, mini-batch gradient descent for neural rankers). (2) UCB score adjustment: at serve time, adjust the pre-computed UCB_a by a staleness penalty: adjusted_UCB_a = UCB_a − staleness_penalty(t − last_update) × Δ̂_expected_drift. Staleness penalty can be estimated from historical UCB drift rates. (3) Reward model ensembles: maintain an ensemble of models trained on rolling windows (last 1hr, 4hr, 24hr). At serve time, UCB uses the ensemble variance across time windows as the uncertainty signal — captures temporal uncertainty without re-solving the full ridge system. (4) Separate fast and slow signals: UCB exploration for slowly-varying item features (genre CTR, general popularity) can be updated daily. For rapidly-varying signals (trending items, breaking news), maintain a separate fast-update cache updated every 10 minutes, bypassing the batched bandit. Practical recommendation: implement 1-hour batches with Sherman-Morrison incremental updates for LinUCB; switch to direct neural feature extraction + LinTS on last layer to amortize full retraining to daily.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Explain cascade bandits. How does position bias complicate exploration in recommendation ranking?',
        a: 'Cascade model: the user scans the ranked list from position 1 downward. At each position they "click" (with probability = attractiveness of item) or "skip" (with probability 1 − attractiveness). They stop after the first click. Consequently: (1) Only the clicked item and all skipped items above it are observed — items below the click are not observed (truncated feedback). (2) Position bias: items at position 1 receive far more examination probability than items at position 5. A CTR of 2% at position 5 may be "better" than 5% at position 1 because position 1 has much higher examination. Position bias complicates exploration because: (a) Swapping a new uncertain item from position 10 to position 1 conflates the position boost with the item quality signal — you can\'t tell if the CTR improved because the item is better or because position 1 has more clicks. (b) Exploration slots: to measure true item quality, you must vary positions in a controlled way. E.g., occasionally show item A at position 1 and item B at position 3, then swap — the difference in CTR attributable to the item (not position) is the debiased quality estimate. (c) Inverse propensity weighting for position: reward for item a at position k: r̂ = click / P(examined | position k). P(examined | k) estimated from randomization experiments or eye-tracking. Production solution: two-stage approach — (1) Use position-debiased reward estimates (examination propensity correction) as bandit reward signals. (2) Run exploration by occasionally promoting items from position k>3 to position 1, comparing against standard allocation. Track the debiased CTR lift separately from the position lift.',
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `The central tension in production recommendation bandits is that pure exploitation creates a filter bubble (popular items stay popular; new items are never discovered), while naive exploration tanks short-term CTR metrics. The interview answer that shows operational experience is: dedicate a fixed exploration budget (e.g., 5% of traffic), use content-based priors to warm-start new item UCB posteriors instead of starting from scratch, and use position-debiased reward estimates to avoid conflating position effects with item quality during cascade exploration.`,
  },
  {
    id: 'non_stationary_bandits',
    title: 'Non-Stationary Bandits',
    subtitle: 'Sliding window UCB, discounted UCB, change-point detection, EXP3, REXP3',
    difficulty: 'advanced',
    estimatedMin: 70,
    tags: ['non-stationary', 'sliding window', 'discounted UCB', 'EXP3', 'adversarial', 'change point'],
    summary: `The stochastic MAB assumption of fixed reward distributions is violated in virtually all real production systems: user preferences drift seasonally, advertising CTR decays with creative fatigue, item freshness degrades, and concept drift occurs due to external events. Non-stationary bandits require algorithms that can track changing optima rather than converging to a fixed one. The key design tension is memory length: a long memory provides accurate estimates of stable arms but is slow to detect changes; a short memory detects changes quickly but has high variance. EXP3 handles the adversarial extreme (arbitrary reward changes) by maintaining a hedge over arms, achieving the minimax-optimal O(√(KT)) adversarial regret.`,
    keyPoints: [
      'Failure of standard UCB/TS in non-stationary settings: UCB1 and standard TS accumulate all historical observations — N_a(t) grows monotonically. Confidence bounds shrink as √(ln t / N_a). For a previously dominant arm that degrades, N_a is large, confidence is tight around the old (now stale) mean, and the UCB stays low. The algorithm exploits a historically good arm even as its true mean deteriorates. Regret grows linearly after the change point.',
      'Sliding window UCB (SW-UCB): maintain only the last W observations per arm. After W rounds, old observations are discarded. UCB index = μ̂_a(W) + B√(ln t / min(N_a, W)) where B is a constant and μ̂_a(W) is the mean of the last min(N_a, W) rewards for arm a. Optimal window W ≈ √(T/K) for abrupt changes occurring O(1) times; regret = O(√(KT)).',
      'Discounted UCB (D-UCB): instead of discarding old observations, discount them exponentially: μ̂_a = Σ_{s≤t: a_s=a} γ^(t−s) r_s / Σ_{s≤t: a_s=a} γ^(t−s) where γ ∈ (0,1) is the discount factor. Effective memory length ≈ 1/(1−γ). Confidence bonus uses effective count Ñ_a = Σ γ^(t−s) 1(a_s=a). Smoother adaptation than SW-UCB; gradient ≈ exponential moving average of rewards.',
      'Change-point detection + reset: monitor each arm for a statistically significant change in reward distribution using a CUSUM test or Page-Hinkley test. When a change is detected for arm a, reset its statistics (A_a = λI, b_a = 0, N_a = 0). Algorithms: CUSUM-UCB, GLR-UCB. More principled than window or discounting — only resets when change is detected, not constantly forgetting. But requires tuning detection threshold (false alarm rate vs change detection speed tradeoff).',
      'EXP3 (Exponential-weight algorithm for Exploration and Exploitation): the fundamental adversarial bandit algorithm. Maintain weights w_a for each arm. At round t: (1) Sample arm a_t with probability p_{a,t} = ((1−γ) w_a / Σ w_j) + γ/K. (2) Observe reward r_t. (3) Compute estimated reward x̂_{a,t} = r_t / p_{a_t,t} (importance weighting to correct for arm selection probability). (4) Update: w_{a_t} ← w_{a_t} × exp(γ x̂_{a_t,t} / K). EXP3 achieves E[R_T] ≤ O(√(KT ln K)) against any adversarial sequence — no distributional assumptions required.',
      'EXP3 analysis: the importance-weighted reward x̂ is unbiased (E[x̂_{a,t}|a_t=a] = r_t). The exponential update is a multiplicative weights step — the Hedge algorithm applied to bandit feedback. The ln K term reflects the cost of not knowing which arm will be optimal; it disappears with better arm structure (graph-structured bandits).',
      'REXP3 (Restarting EXP3): for non-stationary adversarial settings with Υ change points, run EXP3 in epochs of length T/Υ — restart the algorithm from uniform weights at each epoch boundary. If Υ is unknown, use a doubling schedule: run epochs of length 1, 2, 4, ..., restart when epoch ends. Achieves O(Υ^{1/3} K^{1/3} T^{2/3}) regret. An alternative: EXP3-R (EXP3 with restarts triggered by change detection).',
      'Practical: seasonality and concept drift detection. In production recommendation/ad systems: (1) Seasonality (weekly, monthly patterns) is predictable — use time-of-day/day-of-week features in contextual bandit rather than treating it as non-stationarity. (2) Gradual drift (creative fatigue, user preference evolution) — use discounted UCB or exponential smoothing. (3) Abrupt change (product launch, viral event, new competitor) — use change-point detection. Monitor Kolmogorov-Smirnov test on reward distribution per arm; alert when KS statistic > threshold.',
      'Detecting when to re-explore vs exploit: signals that re-exploration is needed: (1) Reward model error increases (monitored via held-out calibration). (2) Reward variance across arms decreases (arms converging — may indicate filter bubble, not true convergence). (3) Distribution shift detector triggers (PSI — Population Stability Index > 0.25). (4) External events (detected from business context — new product launch, news event). At trigger, increase exploration rate (raise ε, lower α UCB constant) and schedule a forced exploration burst.',
      'EXP3 vs UCB for non-stationary settings: EXP3 is optimal for adversarial (worst-case) sequences but has weaker √(KT ln K) regret vs UCB\'s O(log T) regret for stationary stochastic settings. In practice, most production problems are "moderately non-stationary" (slow drift, occasional abrupt changes) — not worst-case adversarial. Sliding window or discounted UCB typically outperforms EXP3 because they leverage the stochastic structure when present while maintaining robustness to gradual changes. EXP3 is the right choice when the adversary is strategic (e.g., competitors choosing ad bids to foil your algorithm) or when reward distributions are fundamentally unpredictable.',
    ],
    checkQuestions: [
      {
        q: 'You\'re running UCB on an ad creative rotation with K=5 creatives. Creative A dominated for 3 months, but a competitor launched a similar ad and A\'s CTR dropped from 8% to 2% overnight. How does UCB fail and what algorithm do you switch to?',
        a: 'UCB failure: creative A has been pulled N_A ≈ 90% of rounds × T_days rounds — maybe N_A = 50,000 observations. Its UCB = 0.08 + √(2 ln t / 50000) ≈ 0.08 + 0.014 = 0.094. After the drop, the empirical mean slowly updates: after 1000 new observations of 2% CTR, μ̂_A = (50000×0.08 + 1000×0.02)/51000 ≈ 0.079 — barely moved. UCB remains the highest and creative A continues to be shown. The algorithm is stuck in a local optimum because N_A is huge. Time to adapt: needs O(N_A) new observations to substantially shift μ̂_A — months. Fixes: (1) Immediate: reset creative A\'s statistics (N_A = 0, μ̂_A = flat prior) when the change is detected. Manually trigger exploration mode. (2) Sliding window UCB with W=7 days: only use the last 7 days of observations. After the drop, within 1 day the window-mean for A drops toward 2%, allowing other creatives to compete. (3) Discounted UCB with γ=0.99: effective memory ≈ 100 rounds, so the old 8% estimates fade within days. (4) CUSUM monitoring: run CUSUM on A\'s reward stream; a 6-point drop from 8% to 2% triggers in O(1/Δ²) ≈ 17 observations. Upon detection, reset A\'s statistics. Best production approach: combine CUSUM detection with SW-UCB (windows correct for gradual drift, CUSUM handles abrupt changes).',
      },
      {
        q: 'Explain the EXP3 importance-weighted reward estimate x̂_{a,t} = r_t / p_{a,t}. Why is it unbiased and what is the variance?',
        a: 'Unbiasedness: we need E[x̂_{a,t}] = μ_a for all arms a (the true expected reward). EXP3 selects arm a_t with probability p_{a,t}. Define x̂_{a,t} = r_t / p_{a_t,t} × 1(a_t = a). Then: E[x̂_{a,t}] = E[r_t / p_{a,t} × 1(a_t = a)] = p_{a,t} × E[r|a] / p_{a,t} = μ_a. Unbiased. The importance weighting corrects for the arm selection probability — the reward for arm a is divided by the probability of having selected it, making rare arms\' reward estimates have larger magnitude to compensate for their infrequent selection. Variance: Var(x̂_{a,t}) = E[x̂_{a,t}²] − μ_a². E[x̂²] = p_{a,t} × E[r²|a] / p_{a,t}² = E[r²|a] / p_{a,t} ≤ r_max² / p_{a,t}. If p_{a,t} = γ/K (minimum probability for uniform exploration), then Var ≤ K·r_max² / γ. With K=10 arms and γ=0.1, variance ≤ 100 × r_max². This is very high variance — EXP3 can have noisy reward estimates, especially for arms with small selection probability. The γ exploration ensures a minimum probability floor, bounding variance. This variance is the cost of the adversarial guarantee — no distributional structure to exploit means you must pay for information gathering.',
      },
      {
        q: 'In a content recommendation system with weekly seasonality (weekend vs weekday user preferences differ strongly), is non-stationary bandit the right framing? What would you do differently?',
        a: 'Seasonal patterns like weekly cycles are not true non-stationarity — the reward distribution is predictably cyclic, not drifting. Non-stationary bandit algorithms (sliding window, discounted UCB) would "solve" the weekend-weekday difference by forgetting weekday observations on weekends, but this throws away useful data. Better approach: (1) Contextual bandit with time features: include day_of_week and hour_of_day as context features. LinUCB or neural contextual bandit will learn that arm reward = f(user, item, time_features). The bandit now correctly models the seasonality as a feature of the context, not as non-stationarity. Observations from all days inform θ̂ via the context features. (2) Hierarchical model: fit a weekend-CTR model and weekday-CTR model separately — shared feature weights but separate bias terms. At serve time, use the appropriate model. (3) Save non-stationary algorithms for: genuine concept drift (user preferences changing over months), abrupt external shocks (competitor launch, news events), item lifecycle decay (article freshness). Seasonality is predictable; concept drift is not. The key diagnostic: does reward correlation P(r_Monday | context) correlate with P(r_Monday_next_week | context)? If yes (stable seasonal pattern), use contextual bandit with time features. If correlation degrades over weeks, use non-stationary tracking.',
      },
      {
        q: 'REXP3 achieves O(Υ^{1/3} K^{1/3} T^{2/3}) regret with Υ change points. How does this compare to the lower bound, and what is the cost of not knowing Υ?',
        a: 'Lower bound for non-stationary bandits with Υ change points: Ω(√(ΥKT)) — any algorithm must incur at least this much regret in the worst case. REXP3\'s bound is O(Υ^{1/3} K^{1/3} T^{2/3}). Comparing exponents: lower bound ∝ (ΥKT)^{1/2}; REXP3 ∝ Υ^{1/3} K^{1/3} T^{2/3}. These match when T^{2/3} ≈ T^{1/2}, i.e., for specific regimes of Υ. The standard result is that the optimal minimax rate for Υ changes is O((ΥKT)^{1/2}); REXP3 does not achieve this — there is a gap. Better algorithms: EXP3-R (EXP3 with restarts triggered by change detection) achieves O(√(ΥKT ln K)) which nearly matches the lower bound. Cost of not knowing Υ: if Υ is known, set epoch length exactly to T/Υ. If unknown, use a doubling trick: run epochs of length 1, 2, 4, 8,... and restart at each epoch end. The doubling adds at most a constant factor to regret (O(log T) epoch ends, each with O(epoch_length × K) exploration cost). Practical implication: the cost of uncertainty about Υ is logarithmic, not polynomial — the doubling trick is essentially free. Most production systems should just use doubling restarts rather than trying to estimate Υ; CUSUM-based change detection is an alternative that avoids wasted exploration during stable periods but requires tuning the detection threshold.',
      },
    ],
    takeaway: `Standard UCB/TS becomes dangerously slow to adapt after a change point because the arm with millions of accumulated observations has a confidence interval so tight it never loses the argmax — the algorithm is frozen on a historically dominant arm long after its reward has collapsed. The right production strategy is to combine two complementary mechanisms: CUSUM-based change detection for abrupt shifts (triggers an immediate statistics reset) and sliding window or discounted UCB for gradual drift (continuously forgets stale observations). Predictable seasonality is not non-stationarity — it belongs in contextual features, not in a forgetting mechanism.`,
  },
]
